1. Establish a Trustworthy Application Baseline
Before adding intelligence, fix the engineering problems that can conceal failures.
Do:
- Correct the TypeScript command so it checks the application and tooling projects.
- Fix the resulting TypeScript errors.
- Resolve backend lint issues, distinguishing actual defects from lint configuration issues.
- Clearly separate demo, emulation, and any future production execution modes.
- Ensure synthetic telemetry and demonstration outcomes are visibly identified.
- Stop reporting real execution, successful simulation, or generated reports when only metadata was produced.
- Represent missing measurements as unavailable, not zero.
Complete when: the application passes effective checks, and every displayed success corresponds to something that actually happened.
This is the foundation for trusting every later step.
2. Fix Authentication, Permissions, and Session Isolation
Do this before connecting the application to anything it can control.
Do:
- Restrict organization/workspace/member administration to appropriate administrators.
- Enforce tenant membership on every REST and WebSocket operation.
- Require access tokens, not refresh tokens, for WebSocket authentication.
- Implement revocable refresh sessions, including logout and refresh-token rotation.
- Make the frontend Logout button call the backend.
- Clear cached operational data and realtime state when the user logs out.
- Scope realtime stores to the selected network/workspace.
- Make the interface permission-aware without relying on it for backend enforcement.
- Recheck authorization for long-lived connections when membership or permissions change.
Complete when: automated tests using two organizations and Admin/Read-Only users prove that users cannot see or change unauthorized resources, and logout actually terminates their session.
Do not postpone this until after real routing control.
3. Create a Reproducible SDN Emulation Environment
Build the environment in which NANFO will observe and control actual traffic.
Do:
- Create a Mininet topology using Open vSwitch.
- Include hosts, access switches, distribution switches, and a core.
- Include redundant paths so rerouting can actually relieve a bottleneck.
- Configure realistic link capacities, delays, and queue limits.
- Integrate Ryu using a supported OpenFlow version.
- Establish stable mappings between application device IDs and switch DPIDs/ports.
- Add scripts for starting, resetting, and stopping the environment.
- Verify the controller environment separately from the FastAPI runtime; they do not need to share one Python environment.
- Start small before scaling to the larger campus demonstration.
Complete when: one reproducible startup procedure creates the network, attaches the controller, and allows hosts to exchange traffic.
A 3D campus graph is useful, but it is not a replacement for this environment.
4. Implement Actual Network Discovery and Telemetry
Replace the synthetic adapters with a collector that observes the emulated network.
Do:
- Discover switches, ports, links, and hosts.
- Collect port/flow counters through the controller.
- Calculate throughput from counter differences and elapsed time.
- Calculate link utilization using known link capacity.
- Measure latency using a defined probe method.
- Measure packet loss using suitable counters or end-to-end observations.
- Collect queue occupancy through instrumentation that actually exposes backlog. Do not assume ordinary OpenFlow statistics provide instantaneous queue depth.
- Handle counter resets, missing samples, invalid values, and stale observations.
- Store metric units, observation timestamps, provenance, and quality indicators.
- Support time-range queries and aggregation for historical analysis.
Complete when: generating traffic changes the dashboard metrics, and independent network tools confirm that the displayed values are credible.
Important: first prove that telemetry is correct. An intelligent controller using incorrect observations will make incorrect decisions.
5. Implement Real, Safe Network Actions
Before training DRL, prove that NANFO can execute useful actions without it.
Do:
- Replace the deterministic hypervisor with a real controller adapter.
- Pass complete action plans, including targets, flow matches, paths, and rate parameters.
- Implement path rerouting.
- Implement supported multipath/group behavior where needed.
- Implement traffic shaping using appropriate queue configuration.
- Implement traffic policing using supported meters or equivalent mechanisms.
- Implement QoS classification and bandwidth constraints.
- Validate topology and device capabilities before dispatch.
- Use acknowledgments/barriers and readback to verify changes.
- Capture the previous relevant configuration.
- Implement actual rollback and verify restoration.
- Prevent concurrent conflicting changes.
- Add route-change limits and hold-down intervals.
Complete when: a manual action from the application changes actual traffic behavior, and a failed change can restore the previous forwarding configuration.
Shaping, policing, and rerouting are different operations. Each needs its own implementation and verification.
6. Make Execution Durable and Recoverable
Real control requires stronger guarantees than a successful API response.
Do:
- Use an atomic state transition or lock to claim an execution.
- Enforce scoped idempotency for repeated requests.
- Persist events reliably alongside state changes, using an outbox or equivalent design.
- Recover unacknowledged Redis messages after worker failures.
- Make consumers safely repeatable.
- Separate command execution from ordinary HTTP request handling where appropriate.
- Define timeouts and cancellation behavior.
- On restart, reconcile in-progress operations with actual controller/switch state.
- Fix the simulation-start publication race.
- Make WebSocket delivery work across application processes, or explicitly constrain deployment until it does.
Complete when: restarting the backend or worker during an operation does not silently lose the operation, execute it twice, or leave the application claiming the wrong network state.
7. Implement a Real DRL Environment and Baselines
Now build the learning problem around the working network.
Do:
- Define the decision interval.
- Define the observation vector and topology representation.
- Include previous actions or observation history where needed.
- Normalize features consistently without hiding absolute offered load.
- Define legal actions and how they map to actual controller operations.
- Define reward terms for delay, loss, throughput, utilization, and route-change penalties.
- Implement environment reset, step, termination, and measurement-window behavior.
- Create deterministic workload scenarios.
- Implement an OSPF baseline using an actual routing implementation.
- Add a simple load-aware heuristic baseline for debugging.
Complete when: a non-learning policy can repeatedly run episodes, take actions, and receive measured next states and rewards.
Do not train the neural network until this loop is reliable.
8. Implement and Train the Actor-Critic Model
This is the central intelligence currently missing.
Do:
- Select one actor-critic algorithm and implement it properly. PPO is a reasonable starting candidate, but the choice should fit the final action space.
- Build the actor and critic in PyTorch.
- Implement rollout collection, losses, updates, and checkpointing.
- Support training and inference as separate operating modes.
- Track rewards, losses, action frequency, and constraint violations.
- Train across different traffic patterns, not just one favorable scenario.
- Use separate training, validation, and held-out evaluation scenarios.
- Save the model, normalization parameters, action mapping, and configuration together.
- Implement policy loading and version tracking.
Complete when: a saved policy can be loaded into a fresh process and make reproducible, meaningful decisions from measured network state.
A changing reward curve is not sufficient. Confirm that the learned actions actually affect the network beneficially.
9. Implement the Neuro-Adaptive Safety Mechanism
Do not attach an unconstrained learned policy directly to network control.
There are two distinct pieces to address:
- Learning stability: whether the chosen adaptive update behaves as intended.
- Action safety: whether a proposed network action is admissible before execution.
Do:
- Define the precise stability property you are targeting.
- Establish the assumptions under which it applies.
- Implement the relevant Lyapunov/drift or neuro-adaptive calculations.
- Define constraints for capacity, permitted paths, QoS, connectivity, and action frequency.
- Evaluate every proposed action before dispatch.
- Reject or modify inadmissible actions.
- Provide a deterministic safe fallback.
- Handle stale telemetry, controller delay, and overloaded networks explicitly.
- Test repeated rejected actions and recovery.
- Record why the filter accepted, changed, or rejected each proposal.
Complete when: deliberately unsafe proposals are blocked or corrected, and the chosen stability claim is justified under explicit assumptions.
A weight clamp, a confidence threshold, or a successful simulation flag alone is not the promised neuro-adaptive filter. Documentation writing can wait; the mathematical basis cannot.
10. Connect the Autonomous Control Loop
Combine the pieces into the actual orchestrator.
The operating loop should be:
Observe network
    -> validate measurement freshness and quality
    -> construct model state
    -> infer proposed action
    -> enforce safety constraints
    -> obtain required approval
    -> execute action
    -> verify switch and traffic state
    -> observe outcome
    -> record decision and feedback
Do:
- Add monitor-only, recommendation, and autonomous modes.
- Start with inference-only deployment of a trained policy.
- Keep online learning disabled until its safety and recovery behavior are established.
- Correlate observations, policy versions, actions, verification, and outcomes.
- Prevent feedback loops that repeatedly undo previous decisions.
- Add degraded behavior for missing telemetry or unavailable controllers.
- Add a clear emergency stop.
Complete when: a controlled congestion scenario causes NANFO to intervene without a manual API call, with an observable and verified result.
To call it proactive, demonstrate intervention before your defined congestion condition, rather than merely reacting after loss occurs.
11. Replace the Simulation Placeholder with a Real Evaluator
Keep the current simulation UI and lifecycle structure, but replace its underlying behavior.
Do:
- Run scenarios against an isolated emulation instance or a validated simulation backend.
- Supply actual topology, workload, action, duration, and seed inputs.
- Generate measured or explicitly modeled outcomes.
- Support meaningful pause/resume/branch behavior.
- Compare results produced under equivalent conditions.
- Bind predeployment results to the exact proposed action and relevant network state.
- Expire stale validation results.
- Never equate message-queue availability with simulation safety.
Complete when: changing a scenario or proposed action changes computed/measured outcomes, and a failed evaluation genuinely blocks the corresponding deployment.
A full RF/mobility physics engine is not necessary to finish the original wired-network proposal. Do not let that expansion delay the real congestion-control evaluator.
12. Finish the Operator Experience
Once real data and actions exist, expose them clearly.
Add:
- Time-series charts for the five telemetry metrics.
- Network-wide and per-link congestion views.
- Observed active flow/path visualization.
- Before/after action comparisons.
- A decision history with policy version and evidence.
- Safety-filter interventions and reasons.
- Model status and inference latency.
- Configuration of supported reward/policy parameters.
- Explicit manual override with scope, duration, reason, and cancellation.
- A visible return-to-autonomy control.
- Clear distinction between measured, modeled, and synthetic information.
Fix:
- Cross-page topology links.
- Snapshot reconciliation after reconnect and dropped events.
- Stale alert and simulation views.
- Network/account state isolation.
- Destructive group-replacement defaults.
- Persisted model loading after refresh.
- Missing pagination and partial-result indicators.
- Mobile layout and keyboard accessibility.
Complete when: an operator can understand what NANFO observed, what it did, why it did it, whether it helped, and how to stop or override it.
13. Complete Reports, Alerts, and Remaining Modules
Finish the features already presented as part of the application.
Reports:
- Generate real CSV and PDF files from scoped data.
- Calculate checksums and sizes from actual file bytes.
- Store artifacts durably.
- Provide authorized download and report history.
- Include workload, policy, metrics, actions, and comparison results.
Alerts:
- Generate alerts from actual network conditions.
- Add persistence thresholds and hysteresis to avoid alert storms.
- Correlate recovery events with the original alert.
- Scope deduplication to the correct tenant/resource.
- Keep REST and realtime state consistent.
Plugins:
- Decide whether they are a registry or executable extensions.
- If executable, implement real package integrity verification, loading, isolation, resource limits, and uninstall.
- Do not label declared permissions as an enforced sandbox.
Energy control:
- If retaining the power-saving promise, implement safe redundant-link/port power management.
- Preserve connectivity and recovery behavior.
- Evaluate it using a defensible energy model or measurement source.
- Do not claim measured power savings from an emulator that only toggles logical state.
Complete when: each visible module performs its advertised operation, not just records a successful status.
14. Run Network-Level Acceptance Testing
Application unit tests are necessary but insufficient.
Build an automated campaign covering:
- Normal low-load traffic.
- Gradually increasing demand.
- Sudden traffic bursts.
- Sustained overload.
- Multiple simultaneous bottlenecks.
- Link failure and recovery.
- Stale/missing telemetry.
- Controller disconnection.
- Worker/backend restart.
- Unsafe policy proposals.
- Manual override.
- Rollback failure.
- Different traffic classes.
- Larger topologies.
Compare OSPF, the heuristic baseline, DRL alone, and DRL with the safety mechanism where appropriate.
Measure:
Area	Measurements
Performance	Latency, goodput/throughput, PDR
Congestion	Frequency, duration, queue occupancy
Utilization	Link loading and capacity-normalized efficiency
Stability	Route-change rate, oscillation, packet reordering
Control overhead	Inference time, action time, CPU/memory, control traffic
Safety	Violations, filter interventions, rollback outcomes
Application	API behavior, realtime recovery, permissions, mobile usability
Complete when: repeated held-out runs meet predefined acceptance criteria, with raw results retained.
“Way better” should mean measurably better under specified conditions without unacceptable regressions, not an assumption that DRL must win every test.
15. Package the Complete Application
Finally, make the finished system reproducible and recoverable.
Do:
- Package the frontend, backend, workers, controller, and required services.
- Pin compatible dependency versions.
- Keep privileged emulation/controller components isolated from the public web application.
- Automate fresh-database migrations.
- Add meaningful readiness checks.
- Require proper secret configuration.
- Keep datastore interfaces private.
- Add backup and restore procedures.
- Bound telemetry/artifact retention.
- Monitor failures, queues, controller connectivity, and model health.
- Verify deployment from an empty environment.
Complete when: a fresh installation can start the full system, run a real congestion-control scenario, survive a restart, and restore from backup.
Recommended Milestones
Milestone	Steps	What You Can Honestly Claim
A. Trustworthy foundation	1–2	The application has meaningful quality checks and secure access boundaries
B. Real network control	3–6	NANFO observes, changes, verifies, and recovers actual emulated network behavior
C. Working intelligence	7–10	A trained, constrained policy autonomously controls network traffic
D. Complete operator application	11–13	Simulation, dashboard, reports, alerts, and controls perform real operations
E. Validated release	14–15	The system is reproducible, benchmarked, deployable, and recoverable
Start with Steps 1–2, then prioritize Steps 3–6. The first major breakthrough should be:
Traffic generated in Mininet appears as measured telemetry in NANFO; an action submitted through NANFO changes the actual packet path; the application verifies the result and can restore the previous configuration.
Once that works reliably, the DRL model has a real system to learn from and control. Until then, additional AI labels or visual enhancements will not close the main application gap.